---
import CaseLayout from '../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../components/f/CaseHeader.astro';
import NextStory from '../components/f/NextStory.astro';

const pct = (v: number, of: number) => `${((v / of) * 100).toFixed(3)}%`;

const facts = [
  { k: 'Role', v: 'Design systems engineering, token architecture, AI tooling and governance' },
  { k: 'Date', v: '2025 – ongoing' },
  { k: 'Client', v: 'Deloitte Converge for Restaurants' },
  { k: 'Design system in Figma', v: 'Grecia Gabriel and Rebecca Bradley' },
];
const overview = [
  { k: 'Challenge', v: 'We had a solid design system in Figma, but it was bypassed the moment PMs prototyped with AI tools: it lived where those tools couldn’t reach.' },
  { k: 'Contribution', v: 'React implementation of the library, token architecture, a published npm package, a component playground and the governance model for AI tooling.' },
  { k: 'Constraints', v: 'NDA-bound enterprise context; components must render safely anywhere; rules must be unambiguous enough for an agent to follow.' },
  { k: 'Outcome', v: 'A published package and Figma Make Kit in use across the project’s pricing line, and an independent review layer that keeps AI-generated UI on-system.' },
];
const numbers = [
  { v: '2', k: 'AI entry points, one governance model' },
  { v: '3', k: 'independent auditors on every component' },
  { v: '2.37:1', k: 'the warning contrast we caught; WCAG asks for 4.5:1' },
];
const decisions = [
  { t: 'Brand color is scarce', b: 'The Deloitte green is reserved for the single most important action and its focus rings; active and selected states use separate tokens. Encoding that in tokens lets an agent apply it without ambiguity.' },
  { t: 'Measured contrast', b: 'The warning color looked legible by eye, yet measured 2.37:1 against WCAG’s 4.5:1 threshold. Warning text now uses a darker token that passes, enforced by an automated review layer.' },
  { t: 'The system doesn’t touch what it doesn’t own', b: 'Components render in unfinished prototypes and legacy apps full of CSS, so the package never styles raw HTML globally. No bare h1, body or button selectors; each component ships its own scoped reset.' },
  { t: 'Tokens are contracts', b: 'Token names map directly to Figma, quirks included. The designer reading the library and the engineer or agent reading the code see the same concepts. Changing a token without agreement breaks something people already built on.' },
];
---

<CaseLayout
  title="A design system for humans and AI agents — William Montoya"
  description="How I turned a design system that AI demos were bypassing into a code package, and a governance model, that humans and agents both build on."
  readFull="7 min"
  readTldr="1 min"
>
  <CaseHeader
    lead="Operationalizing a design system for"
    accent="humans and AI agents"
    question="How do you make a design system that AI tools and agents actually use, instead of approximating it?"
    facts={facts}
    overview={overview}
    readFull="7 min"
    readTldr="1 min"
  />

  <figure class="figure figure--lead wrap" data-reveal>
    <div class="stage" style="aspect-ratio:1296/640">
      <div class="browser" style={`left:${pct(148, 1296)};top:${pct(56, 640)};width:${pct(1000, 1296)}`}>
        <div class="browser__bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
        <img src="/assets/f/converge-ds.webp" width="1440" height="810" alt="The Converge design system playground: the color foundations, with brand, neutral, text and selection tokens." fetchpriority="high" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <section class="chapter chapter--section wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-numbers" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-numbers" class="t-h3 split__label">In numbers</h2>
      <div class="columns3 numbers split__body">
        {numbers.map((n) => (
          <div><p class="t-display">{n.v}</p><p class="t-muted">{n.k}</p></div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-problem" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-problem" class="t-h3 split__label">The problem</h2>
      <div class="prose measure split__body">
        <p>The design team already had a mature system in Figma, built by Grecia Gabriel and Rebecca Bradley.</p>
        <p>The problem appeared when product managers started using Claude Code and Figma Make to explore ideas with clients. The system dropped out of the conversation because it lived where those tools could not reach.</p>
        <p>Exploration got much faster. Consistency dropped just as quickly. Demos no longer shared the system’s visual language, interaction patterns or UX principles. Each PM improvised their own interface, and the design system became a reference file AI never touched.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter chapter--tight wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-why" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-why" class="t-h3 split__label">Why a strong system wasn’t enough</h2>
      <div class="chapter__stack split__body">
        <div class="prose measure">
          <p>Figma Make can bring a real design system into AI-generated prototypes through Make Kits: a component package, the tokens and styles from a Figma library, and usage guidelines, so generated screens use the real system instead of an approximation.</p>
          <p>The catch: a Make Kit can’t be built from a Figma file. It needs a published React package first. Only then can it become part of a Make Kit, and only then can guidelines be layered on top.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-h2 pull">The system was strong in Figma, but nothing AI used could consume it. It needed to become code.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="figure wrap" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="stage" style="aspect-ratio:1296/680">
      <img class="flat" src="/assets/f/converge-button.webp" width="1600" height="1412" loading="lazy" alt="Button component in the playground: variants, sizes, icons, states and the bulk-action bar." style={`left:${pct(298, 1296)};top:${pct(48, 680)};width:${pct(700, 1296)}`} />
    </div>
  </figure>

  <section class="chapter wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-contribution" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-contribution" class="t-h3 split__label">My contribution</h2>
      <div class="chapter__stack split__body">
        <div class="prose measure">
          <p>I took the Figma system and translated it into a working code package so Make Kits and coding agents could use it directly. That covered the React component library, token architecture, an npm release, and a playground to validate each component before opening it to other teams.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-h2 pull">Building the code was the vehicle. Governing it was the real challenge.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter chapter--section wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-decisions" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-decisions" class="t-h3 split__label">The decisions that hold it together</h2>
      <div class="chapter__stack split__body">
        <p class="measure" data-depth="full">A package consumed by agents only works if its rules don’t depend on human judgment. A skilled designer can navigate ambiguity; an agent follows its rules literally and fills any gap with a guess. So the rules had to be right before they were exposed.</p>
        <div class="grid2">
          {decisions.map((d) => (
            <div><h3 class="t-h3">{d.t}</h3><p class="t-muted">{d.b}</p></div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="figure wrap" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="stage" style="aspect-ratio:1296/600">
      <img class="flat" src="/assets/f/converge-metriccard.webp" width="1600" height="1171" loading="lazy" alt="MetricCard and InformativeCard components with status badges." style={`left:${pct(318, 1296)};top:${pct(44, 600)};width:${pct(660, 1296)}`} />
    </div>
    <figcaption class="t-small">Each status maps to a dedicated token, so an agent applies “On track” or “Watch” deterministically</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="chapter wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-org" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-org" class="t-h3 split__label">Governing exploration</h2>
      <div class="prose measure split__body">
        <p>PMs needed to explore ideas with clients using AI without turning those explorations into inconsistent experiences that are hard to maintain.</p>
        <p>We gave exploration structure: a controlled environment, real production components in a playground, clear principles, and a review cycle where explored concepts return to design before reaching production.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter chapter--tight wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-entry" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-entry" class="t-h3 split__label">One system, two entry points</h2>
      <div class="chapter__stack split__body">
        <div class="grid2">
          <div>
            <h3 class="t-h3">Figma Make</h3>
            <p class="t-muted">The Make Kit connects our published npm package, Figma tokens and usage rules directly inside Figma Make. PMs prototype with the same components engineers ship.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="t-h3">Claude Code</h3>
            <p class="t-muted">Through a CLAUDE.md with the architecture and hard rules, plus reusable skills for building, validating and documenting components. Three read-only auditors review every component in parallel: tokens, accessibility with measured contrast, and QA.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="t-h2 pull">Generation is fast and confidently wrong. Discipline lives in a review layer with no incentive to approve its own work.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="figure wrap" data-depth="full" data-reveal>
    <div class="stage" style="aspect-ratio:1296/560">
      <img class="flat" src="/assets/f/converge-sidebar.webp" width="1600" height="796" loading="lazy" alt="Sidebar component in light expanded and dark collapsed states." style={`left:${pct(248, 1296)};top:${pct(52, 560)};width:${pct(800, 1296)}`} />
    </div>
  </figure>

  <section class="chapter chapter--section wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-outcome" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-outcome" class="t-h3 split__label">Early outcomes</h2>
      <div class="prose measure split__body">
        <p>The pricing line now builds with the Make Kit, the npm package and shared rules for Claude Code, backed by knowledge-transfer sessions with the teams. Those same rules are the base of a design lint we’re building.</p>
        <p>With those rules, PMs on the pricing line iterate on designs close to final, and my time goes where it should: cognitive load, efficiency and information architecture.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter chapter--section wrap" aria-labelledby="ch-reflection" data-reveal>
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="ch-reflection" class="t-h3 split__label">Reflection</h2>
      <div class="split__body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-10)">
        <p class="t-h2 pull">Once anyone can generate a screen in seconds, the work moves from drawing screens to encoding the rules that keep them coherent.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <NextStory current="/converge-design-system" />
</CaseLayout>
